#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# set_precision

#% text
# `set_precision(x, d)` gives `x` with every number as a ball of `d` significant digits.

#% text
# `set_precision(x, inf)` makes every number exact.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Machine reals are converted by their exact binary value, so `set_precision(0.1, 30)` shows the binary error of `0.1`. Exact numbers become balls of `d` digits.

#% item
# Use `n(x, d)` to evaluate an expression to `d` digits instead.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
set_precision(0.1, 30)
#% output
#= 0.100000000000000005551115123126

#% cell
set_precision(1/3, 20)
#% output
#= 0.33333333333333333333

#% cell
set_precision(0.5, inf)
#% output
#= 1/2

#% cell
set_precision(0.1, inf)
#% output
#= 3602879701896397/36028797018963968

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Every number of an expression:

#% cell
set_precision(2.0*x + 1/3, 10)
#% output
#= 0.333333333 + 2.000000000*x

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Compared with `n`, which computes `1/10` to the requested digits:

#% cell
[set_precision(0.1, 25), n(1/10, 25)]
#% output
#= [0.1000000000000000055511151, 0.10000000000000000000000000000]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `precision`, `accuracy`, `n`, `rationalize`
